 :org/entity-kind :named-org
 :org/category :cat-sports
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :global
 :org/region-primary :region-europe
 :org/founding-date 1904
 :org/instance-count-estimate 211
 :org/member-count-basis "source-pinned as STALE: the 265 million players figure (270M including referees and officials) that is still quoted everywhere comes from FIFA's Big Count 2006, a survey of the then-207 member associations, first run in 2000. No superseding global count surfaced in a 2026 search. Twenty years on, that number should be treated as historical, so no current :org/member-count is asserted — 211 member associations is the only present-day figure."
 :org/legal-registration :registered
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Global federation layer whose base is hundreds of thousands of unregistered village and workplace teams."
 :org/source-name "FIFA Big Count 2006 (FIFA Communications Division / Information Services)"
 :org/source-url "https://digitalhub.fifa.com/m/55621f9fdc8ea7b4/original/mzid0qmguixkcmruvema-pdf.pdf"
 :org/source-date "2006"
 :org/source-tier :primary
 :org/source-caveat "Deliberately pinned to expose staleness: the world's most-quoted participation statistic for the world's largest sport is two decades old."
 :org/pinned-on "2026-07-25"
 :org/refresh-checked "2026-07-26"
 :org/refresh-result "Searched again for a successor to Big Count 2006. None found. FIFA now publishes a Professional Football Report instead — 123,694 PROFESSIONAL players worldwide as of 2023 — which counts the smallest and most legible slice of the game. Country-level 'most players' lists circulating for 2025 are unattributed social-media compilations, not federation data. So the trend is not that the count was updated but that the measurement moved to the professional layer, leaving the amateur and unregistered base uncounted since 2006."
 :org/data-provenance "source-pinned"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

